    "title": "Hypothetical: Assessing Local Model Reproducibility - What Needs Recording?",
    "body": "I'm exploring a hypothetical scenario for comparing local language models, specifically focusing on reproducibility. The core question is: what technical details are essential to record to ensure a generated output from a specific local model can be reliably reproduced later? [F1]\n\nMy current thinking is that simply noting the model name isn't enough. We'd need to meticulously log the exact model version, the precise prompt used, all generation settings (like temperature, top-p, repetition penalties), and the underlying hardware configuration (CPU, GPU, RAM, VRAM). This level of detail seems crucial if we ever want to compare outcomes between different setups or even within the same setup over time.\n\nThis is purely a hypothetical discussion at this stage, as no actual experimental results are available. [F3] I'm interested in hearing from others on what other parameters or system-level details might be critical for achieving true reproducibility in a local LLM context. What else should be on this checklist?",
    "fact_refs": ["F1", "F2", "F3"],
    "plan": "Draft a Reddit post discussing the hypothetical need for detailed recording of parameters to ensure reproducibility when comparing local LLMs. Explicitly state the hypothetical nature and lack of experimental results. List potential parameters to record based on F2. Keep the word count within the brief. Ensure the title is restrained and the body focuses on the question of reproducibility. Invite focused discussion."
